  "response": "I'd strengthen **`crime_00008`** first. It's the only weak-test finding that covers an esbuild plugin, and it has no assertions at all.\n\n## The finding\n\nFrom `test/index.test.ts`, charge **\"Test That Proves Nothing\"**, claim `no_assertions`, severity `medium`, confidence `0.88`:\n\n> - `\"test: \\\"native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly\\\"\"`\n> - `\"0 expect/assert calls\"`\n> - `\"lines 537-548\"`\n> - `\"blast radius scored by the code under test: src/utils.ts\"`\n\nThe summary says: *\"contains no expect/assert calls. A test that only runs setup gives agents false confidence.\"*\n\nThe other `no_assertions` findings (`crime_00011`, `00012`, `00013`, `00027`, `00028`) all cover `--dts`, graphql or tsconfig builds, not esbuild plugins. So this is the one that fits your regression.\n\n## Why it lets a regression through\n\nI read the test in the fixture:\n\n```ts\ntest('native-node-module plugin should handle *.node(.js) import properly', async () => {\n  await run(\n    getTestName(),\n    {\n      'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n      'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n    },\n    { entry: ['input.tsx'] },\n  )\n})\n```\n\nIt discards what `run()` returns, so it only fails if the build throws. The plugin is `src/esbuild/native-node-module.ts`. In this test, `onResolve` matches `./hi.node`, and `require.resolve` returns `hi.node.js`. That path doesn't end in `.node`, so the plugin returns `{ path: resolvedId }` and the file stays in the normal `file` namespace, where it gets bundled inline. Two kinds of regression would still pass:\n- The `.js` file gets sent into the `node-file` namespace. It would then become a runtime `require(...)` wrapped in `try { … } catch {}`, which fails silently.\n- The `.node` → `file` loader gets applied to the `.js` file, which would emit it as a separate asset.\n\nNeither of these makes the build throw.\n\n## The assertion I'd add\n\nThe test right before it in the same file captures `output` and `outFiles`, so I'd follow that pattern:\n\n```ts\nconst { output, outFiles } = await run(\n  getTestName(),\n  {\n    'input.tsx': `export * from './hi.node'`,\n    'hi.node.js': `export const hi = 'hi'`,\n  },\n  { entry: ['input.tsx'] },\n)\n// hi.node.js must be bundled inline, not emitted as a separate file\nexpect(outFiles).toEqual(['input.js'])\n// and its export must actually be in the bundle, not wrapped in a runtime require\nexpect(output).toContain(`hi = \"hi\"`)\nexpect(output).not.toContain('hi.node')\n```\n\nThe key line is `outFiles` equals `['input.js']`, because it catches the `file` loader leaking onto the `.js` file. The `toContain` / `not.toContain` checks catch the namespace mistake. I haven't run this, and esbuild's exact output formatting may need adjusting. If it's flaky, add `expect(output).toMatchSnapshot()` like the test before it does.\n\n## One caveat about the scan itself\n\nThe evidence line `\"blast radius scored by the code under test: src/utils.ts\"` points at the wrong file. The code under test is `src/esbuild/native-node-module.ts`. That means its blast-radius score (`0.45`, `\"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 18`) comes from the shared utils module, not the plugin. The ranking is still right, but those numbers don't describe how widely the plugin is used.\n\nA test for the other half of the plugin would also help: a real `.node` binary should end up as a file asset and be loaded through `require`. Right now nothing tests that.",
